    val extended=listOf(
        ReadingLesson("shade","The Coolest Place to Learn", """
            On a hot afternoon, students at a secondary school discovered that the concrete playground was almost empty. Everyone was standing beside a narrow line of trees. Their geography teacher, Ms Chen, asked a question: could the students measure the difference they already felt? A group borrowed thermometers and planned to compare the playground with a shaded corner. They expected the experiment to take one afternoon, but designing a fair comparison proved harder than they thought.

            The first group measured the playground at noon and the shaded area an hour later. Their results looked impressive, until another student pointed out that the weather had changed between the two measurements. They started again, this time working in pairs at both locations at the same moment. They also kept the instruments the same height above the ground and repeated the process on five afternoons. The shaded area was consistently cooler, although the size of the difference varied with cloud and wind conditions.

            The students presented their records to the head teacher. Instead of immediately asking for more trees, they explained what their measurements could and could not establish. They had studied air temperature, not how comfortable every person felt, and they had not investigated the cost of maintaining new plants. The head teacher appreciated this caution and invited the school gardener to discuss suitable species with them. Together they proposed a small planting trial rather than a complete redesign.

            Months later, the first young trees were growing beside the playground. They did not yet provide much shade. Nevertheless, the students regarded the project as a success. They had learned that useful evidence often comes from correcting an attractive first answer. For Ms Chen, the most valuable result was a new habit: before making a confident claim, her students now asked whether their comparison was fair.
        """.trimIndent(),mapOf("concrete" to "混凝土", "shaded" to "有树荫的", "consistently" to "一贯地", "caution" to "谨慎", "trial" to "试验"),listOf(
            q("shade:1","细节题","What was wrong with the first comparison?","The students used trees that were too young|The two places were measured at different times|The instruments were all broken|The playground had already been redesigned",1,"首轮在正午和一小时后分别测量，期间天气改变；第二轮才在同一时刻测量两地。"),
            q("shade:2","推断题","Why did the head teacher appreciate the students' caution?","It saved her from reading their records|It proved that planting trees would cost nothing|It showed that they understood the limits of their evidence|It meant the students had abandoned the project",2,"学生区分了气温与个人舒适度，并承认未研究养护费用，说明他们不把有限证据夸大为完整结论。"),
            q("shade:3","主旨题","What is the main purpose of the passage?","To show how an investigation taught students to evaluate evidence|To argue that every playground needs a complete redesign|To explain why thermometers are unreliable|To compare the prices of different tree species",0,"种树是情境，文章重点是发现不公平比较、修正方法和认识证据局限，末句明确落在这种新习惯。"),
            q("shade:4","词义猜测题","What does 'establish' in paragraph 3 mean?","Decorate|Plant|Pay for|Demonstrate with evidence",3,"measurements could and could not establish 后文列出已测气温与尚未证明的舒适度及成本，establish 指以证据确立。"),
            q("shade:5","指代题","What does 'They' at the beginning of the last paragraph refer to?","The instruments|The young trees|The school records|The weather conditions",1,"前句主语是 first young trees；它们尚幼，所以不能提供很多树荫，They 指幼树。"))),
        ReadingLesson("voices","A Device That Learns Who Is Speaking", """
            When an engineering club designed a voice-controlled noticeboard, its members tested it with their own voices. It worked almost every time. They imagined that students could ask about events without touching a screen. At a demonstration, however, an older visitor repeated the same question three times and received three different answers. One club member suggested that the visitor should speak more like the people who had made the device.

            Their adviser challenged that suggestion. A public noticeboard, she argued, should adapt to its users rather than expect every user to sound alike. The club invited volunteers of different ages and from different neighbourhoods to try the system. Several spoke quietly; others paused between words or pronounced familiar words differently. The new recordings revealed weaknesses that the original tests had completely missed. High accuracy within a small group had created a misleading sense of confidence.

            Improving the device raised another question: what should happen to people's recordings? Some volunteers were happy to help but did not want their voices kept indefinitely. The club rewrote its plan. Volunteers could choose whether to contribute a recording, and could later ask for it to be removed. Basic commands would be processed on the device whenever possible. The students also added a large button so that a person who could not use voice control could still obtain the same information.

            The revised noticeboard was less impressive in one respect: it sometimes admitted that it had not understood a question. Yet its users preferred a clear request to repeat over an answer that sounded certain but was wrong. The club began reporting results for several groups separately instead of presenting one overall number. Its members no longer described their invention as a machine that understood everyone. They described it as a system they were learning to test more honestly. The failed demonstration had changed both their product and their definition of success.
        """.trimIndent(),mapOf("noticeboard" to "公告板", "adapt" to "适应", "misleading" to "误导的", "indefinitely" to "无限期地", "revised" to "修改后的"),listOf(
            q("voices:1","细节题","How were recordings handled in the revised plan?","Every visitor had to provide one|They were sold to support the club|Volunteers could ask for their recordings to be removed|They could never be used for improvement",2,"第三段明确 volunteers could...later ask for it to be removed，参与自愿且可以撤回。"),
            q("voices:2","推断题","What was the main weakness of the original tests?","They used a group too similar to the designers|They included too many different neighbourhoods|They relied only on written questions|They tested the large button too often",0,"开始只用开发者自己的声音测试，后来的多年龄和多口音测试才暴露问题，可推断原样本缺乏代表性。"),
            q("voices:3","主旨题","Which title best expresses the message?","How to Make Visitors Speak Clearly|A New Way to Sell Recordings|Why Voice Control Must Replace Buttons|Designing for Users Means Testing Beyond Yourself",3,"文章主线从只测试自己，到兼顾不同人、隐私与替代按钮，强调面向真实用户的包容性测试。"),
            q("voices:4","词义猜测题","What does 'admitted' in the last paragraph mean?","Recorded in secret|Acknowledged openly|Allowed someone inside|Announced a school event",1,"装置有时表示没听懂，与假装确定却回答错误形成对比，admitted 指公开承认局限。"),
            q("voices:5","指代题","What does 'it' in 'ask for it to be removed' refer to?","The noticeboard|The large button|A volunteer's recording|The adviser's suggestion",2,"前文谈自愿贡献 a recording，随后可以请求删除，因此 it 指某人的录音。"))),
        ReadingLesson("borrowing","The Shelf of Things You Can Borrow", """
            A public library once asked residents what they needed besides books. The replies included sewing machines, camping equipment and tools that people might use only a few times a year. Buying these things was expensive, and storing them could be difficult in small homes. The library decided to lend a limited range of objects for a six-month trial. Its staff were excited, but the idea was not welcomed by everyone.

            One resident worried that borrowers would return broken equipment. Another thought that lending objects would distract librarians from helping readers. Rather than dismiss these concerns, the library began with just ten items. Borrowers received brief instructions, and staff checked each object before and after a loan. The library also arranged short workshops led by experienced residents. People could learn a skill before taking unfamiliar equipment home, reducing the chance that a useful tool would simply sit unused.

            At first, staff measured success by counting loans. Then they met a woman who had borrowed a sewing machine only once. She explained that she had repaired school uniforms for several families during that week. A man who borrowed camping equipment said it had allowed him to try an activity before deciding whether to buy anything. These conversations suggested that the number of loans alone could not capture the service's value. Staff began asking what the borrowed objects had enabled people to do.

            At the end of the trial, some items had needed repairs, and the checking process had taken more time than expected. The library published these difficulties alongside its positive stories. Residents voted to continue the programme, with fewer items that required specialist maintenance. The aim was not to replace local shops or promise that every object could be shared. It was to make occasional access possible without unnecessary ownership. A shelf of ordinary things had started a wider conversation about what a neighbourhood could provide together.
        """.trimIndent(),mapOf("residents" to "居民", "equipment" to "设备", "dismiss" to "不予考虑", "maintenance" to "维护", "ownership" to "所有权"),listOf(
            q("borrowing:1","细节题","How did the library respond to residents' concerns?","It cancelled the trial immediately|It started small and checked equipment carefully|It required every borrower to buy the object|It stopped lending books",1,"第二段描述只从十件物品开始，提供说明并在借出归还时检查，回应损坏和人力方面的担忧。"),
            q("borrowing:2","推断题","What did the woman's story help staff realise?","One loan could benefit more people than the count suggested|Sewing machines were the only useful items|Borrowers preferred to buy every object|Loan numbers proved that repairs were unnecessary",0,"她一次借用为多个家庭修校服，说明借阅次数不能完全反映受益范围与实际价值。"),
            q("borrowing:3","主旨题","What does the passage mainly discuss?","Why libraries should charge more for books|How shops can stop sharing programmes|Why every home needs camping equipment|How a careful borrowing trial explored shared access",3,"全文讲图书馆谨慎试点物品共享、修正评价标准并承认成本，而非无限制共享所有物品。"),
            q("borrowing:4","词义猜测题","What does 'capture' in paragraph 3 mean?","Take away by force|Put into storage|Represent accurately|Break accidentally",2,"上下文把借阅数量与实际帮助对照，数字 alone cannot capture value 指不能准确体现价值。"),
            q("borrowing:5","指代题","What does 'these difficulties' in the last paragraph refer to?","Unpopular books and empty workshops|Repair needs and unexpectedly long checks|The woman's school uniforms|The price of new homes",1,"紧邻前句说 some items needed repairs 和 checking took more time，these difficulties 回指这两项困难。"))),
        ReadingLesson("photograph","Looking Beyond the Photograph", """
            Before visiting an art museum, Daniel saved a photograph of a painting on his phone. He liked its bright colours and expected the original to look exactly the same. Standing in front of the painting, he felt disappointed. Its colours seemed quieter, and the surface was uneven. He checked his phone again, wondering whether the museum had displayed a poor copy. A guide noticed his confusion and invited him to look more slowly.

            First, she asked him to move a few steps sideways. Light reflected differently from the raised marks of paint, revealing small details that the flat photograph had hidden. She then explained that cameras and screens do not simply transfer colour unchanged. Lighting, camera settings and display brightness can all affect what a viewer sees. The image Daniel had saved was useful for recognising the painting, but it was not a complete substitute for the object.

            The guide did not argue that photographs were worthless. They allowed people who lived far away to explore the collection, and helped researchers compare works in different museums. During conservation work, carefully controlled photographs could also reveal changes that the eye might miss. The important question was not whether to trust a photograph absolutely, but what purpose it was suitable for and how it had been produced.

            Daniel returned to the painting after lunch. This time he put away his phone and tried to describe three details he had not noticed earlier. He still preferred some colours in the photograph, but he no longer regarded that preference as proof that something was wrong with the original. On his way home, he changed the note beneath his saved image. Instead of writing that it showed exactly what the painting looked like, he wrote that it was one view of the painting under particular conditions. The difference seemed small, yet it had changed the way he looked at images.
        """.trimIndent(),mapOf("uneven" to "不平整的", "reflected" to "反射", "substitute" to "替代品", "conservation" to "保护修复", "particular" to "特定的"),listOf(
            q("photograph:1","细节题","What did moving sideways help Daniel notice?","That the painting was a copy|That his phone had stopped working|Details in the raised paint surface|A different painting behind him",2,"第二段说明不同角度的反光呈现了颜料凸起的细节，这些细节被平面照片隐藏。"),
            q("photograph:2","推断题","How did Daniel's attitude change?","He learned to treat an image as a view shaped by conditions|He decided that photographs had no educational value|He believed every museum displayed copies|He stopped having personal colour preferences",0,"结尾他把说明改为特定条件下的一个视角，仍保留颜色偏好但不再将偏好当作原作有问题的证据。"),
            q("photograph:3","主旨题","What is the main message?","Museums should forbid all cameras|Images are useful when their purpose and limits are understood|A screen always shows more detail than an original|Personal preference determines whether art is genuine",1,"第三段平衡说明照片用途与局限，末段体现理解媒介条件后的态度变化。"),
            q("photograph:4","词义猜测题","What does 'substitute' in paragraph 2 mean?","A source of brighter light|A written description|A kind of damage|Something used in place of another thing",3,"recognising useful 与 not complete substitute 对照，照片能帮助识别但不能完全替代实体原作。"),
            q("photograph:5","指代题","What does 'They' at the start of paragraph 3 refer to?","The colours|The guides|Photographs|The researchers",2,"前句主语为 photographs，后句说明其让远处的人探索馆藏并帮助研究，They 回指照片。")))
    )
